    "        \"instruction\": \"How do you crack a Caesar cipher in a CTF?\",\n",
    "        \"output\": \"Caesar cipher shifts each letter by a fixed number (1-25). To crack:\\n\\nMethod 1 - Brute force (try all 25 shifts):\\n\\ndef caesar_decrypt(text, shift):\\n    result = ''\\n    for c in text:\\n        if c.isalpha():\\n            shifted = (ord(c.lower()) - ord('a') - shift) % 26\\n            result += chr(shifted + ord('a'))\\n        else:\\n            result += c\\n    return result\\n\\nfor shift in range(26):\\n    print(f'Shift {shift}: {caesar_decrypt(ciphertext, shift)}')\\n\\nMethod 2 - Frequency analysis: Most common letter is likely 'e'. Compare ciphertext frequency to English.\\n\\nMethod 3 - Online tools: CyberChef (gchq.github.io/CyberChef) has ROT13 and Caesar cipher built-in.\"\n",
